## 7. Direction of the selection bias (explicit)
- Observed-wealth rates diverge most in 1996 (white 47.4% vs black 68.3%, Hispanic 66.6%); of the 1985 wealth-observed base, 61.6% of whites vs 80.5% blacks / 80.3% Hispanics are still observed in 1996. If whites who drop out of the wealth module were the poorer ones, the surviving 1996 white sample would be upward-selected and the per-wave 1996 white/black ratio would be INFLATED.
- The data do not support that direction: white 1996 medians are flat across retention groups ($42,600 per-wave, $42,600 full-panel stayers, $42,000 observed-1996-only), while black and Hispanic respondents answering the 1996 wealth module WITHOUT a complete triple are richer than full-panel stayers ($11,918 vs $9,075; $4,500 vs $2,400).
- Conclusion: composition effects in 1996, if anything, UNDERSTATE rather than overstate the white-vs-minority ratio (panel 1996 ratios 4.69 white/black, 17.75 white/Hispanic vs per-wave 4.35 and 16.62). The per-wave widening is not an artefact of differential attrition, but per-wave years remain partly different respondents.

## 8. Robustness of the ratio scale
- The white/Hispanic ratio is NOT scale-robust: the Hispanic median sits one reporting-rounding step above zero ($729 in 1985, $1,921 in 1990, $2,562 in 1996; 34-42% of Hispanic families hold net wealth <= 0). Restricting to strictly positive wealth cuts the white/Hispanic ratio from 7.00 to 2.24 (1985) and from 16.62 to 5.17 (1996), while the dollar gap is stable ($4,375 -> $4,520 in 1985; $40,038 -> $43,000 in 1996). For this contrast the delta-$ and the <=0 share difference are the load-bearing statistics.
- The white/black ratio is less scale-sensitive (2.19 -> 1.59 positive-only in 1985; 4.35 -> 2.69 in 1996), same caveat applies.
- Suspected top-coding (84 obs at $1,321,099 in 1990; 141 at $1,232,183 in 1996 — an interpretation, not a documented fact) moves means, not medians: two-sided 1/99 winsorisation changes the 1996 mean from $114,352 to $114,784 for whites and $45,080 to $40,630 for blacks, while medians are unchanged (a median is invariant to monotone tail clipping); 5%-trimmed medians are also unchanged ($42,600 / $9,800 / $2,500).

